= Acknowledgements <ch:thanks>

After almost five years as a PhD student and more than ten years at the University of Bonn, the time has come to express my gratitude to some great people.

I want to thank Michael Köhl for his supervision and support ever since starting in his group as a young bachelor student.
You allowed me to pursue side projects without a leash, aware that improving the experimental setup was always in the back of my mind.
It was great to know that you completely trusted me in taking care of the experimental setup and that you would have our backs in case things went south.
I had a great time learning about physics directly from you and from others in the environment you provided for us.

I want to thank Andrea Bergschneider for her supervision since the first day she started in our group.
You kept us on track and on our feet to not get lost in some inconsequential details, while starting and managing your own research project.
Your commitment to keeping our group running and in good spirits is really impressive and hope that your efforts will not be in vain.
I will always keep my fingers crossed for you to achieve your personal and scientific goals.

I want to thank Nick Klemmer for the fun years of sharing a lab and an office.
We started together in a terrible situation and really made the most of it, thank you for not quitting during that first year.
It was great that we always felt comfortable making fun of each others mistakes, bringing forth some incredible memes and inside jokes along way.
At last, thank you for always covering for me in those early meetings when I was running late.

I want to thank Valentin Jonas for turning out to be a great student after not regularly attending my atomic physics tutorial many years ago.
Without your math skills we would still be sending experimental parameters to our theory collaborators while desperately trying to evaluate the Floquet data.
Working together with you while listening to every possible version of _Erinner mich_ and counting seconds in Japanese were the most fun times I had in the lab.
Thank you for introducing me to the art of grinding Cookie Clicker and for teaching me all the strategies to play the game as efficiently as possible.
I think we really proved that you can do serious work in a not-so-serious environment.
Outside of the lab, we had a great time watching 7 vs. Wild together.
May your hex keys always be delicious and your Monster always be cold.

I want to thank Moritz Scharfstädt for introducing me to the protein lifestyle and for enjoying the friendly Prime League rivalry between Eintracht Spandau and No Need Orga.
It was always fun chatting with you when we were still working in the office way too late.
Having lunch with you was a blast every time and I will miss your rants about the small servings and questionable vegan dishes at the Mensa.
May your armor always be shiny and your beard never stop growing.

I want to thank Steffen Gohlke for every kilometer we enjoyed together on our road bikes.
From grinding RTFs, to spectating Liège-Bastogne-Liège and Eschborn-Frankfurt, and racing Rund um Köln, we always had a great time suffering together while discussing bike tech or the remaining climbs on the route.
I really hope we can continue on this journey at some point in the future.
May your CdA always be low and your tires always be at the right pressure.

I want to thank the entire research group for the fun time we spent together working, chatting, memeing, solving Sudoku, having lunch, and hanging out inside and outside of the university.
In alphabetic order, these are
Andreas von Haaren,
Andreas Kell,
Arun Bahuleyan,
Daniel Eberz,
Felix Rönchen,
Hugo Zelada,
Jonas Schmitz,
Juliane Reuter,
Julian Spanier,
Marcell Gall,
Martin Link,
Moritz Breyer,
Nicola Wurz,
Om Pote,
Pascal Kobel,
Ralf Berner,
Santhosh Surendra,
Thorsten Langerfeld,
Tobias Grundmann,
and finally Tobias Kree.
Additionally, I want to thank Akos Hoffmann for helping with all kinds of repairs in the lab in record time.
Even though electronic devices are your specialty, you would not shy away from plumbing work and fixing chillers or heat exchangers.
Thank you for always coming to my office after sending an e-mail to make sure it arrived safely, it was always a great opportunity to chat with you.

A special thank you goes out to Lukas Ahlheit and Andreas Redmann.
We met in the bachelor pre-course more than eleven years ago and maintained a great friendship ever since.
Even though we were no longer farming LP together during our time as PhD studends, we still enjoyed our lunch dates at the Mensa, sometimes even outside.
Now that our time together at the IAP is over, I am already looking forward to our next meeting somewhere in Europe.

